import GroupApproximation.Algebra.FinitePresentationTietze

/-!
# The Tietze kernel step with a non-free source

`Algebra/FinitePresentationTietze.isFinitelyNormallyGenerated_ker` proves the
hard half: if `W` is finitely presented then the kernel of **any** surjection
onto it from a free group of finite rank is finitely normally generated -- not
merely the kernel of the presentation one happens to be handed.  That is the
genuine Tietze content, since the hypothesis supplies a presentation on *some*
finite generating set while the conclusion is about the one at hand.

This file adds the one step that turns it into the statement Lemma 54.3 uses,
where the source is not free:

> if `E` and `W` are both finitely presented and `f : E ↠ W` is surjective,
> then `ker f` is finitely normally generated.

This is the converse of Mathlib's `Group.IsFinitelyPresented.of_surjective`, and
Mathlib has only the forward direction.  In the application `E` is the free
permutational precursor `(∗_x K_x) ⋊ G` and `W` is the wreath product, neither
of which is free, so the free-source form cannot be applied directly.

The reduction is short and is the whole file.  Choose a presentation
`φ : FreeGroup (Fin n) ↠ E` of the source -- finite presentability of `E` is
used only for the surjection, not for its relators.  Then `f ∘ φ` is a surjection
from a free group of finite rank onto `W`, so its kernel is finitely normally
generated by the free-source theorem.  That kernel is `(ker f).comap φ`, and
pushing it forward along the surjective `φ` recovers `ker f` exactly, while
`Subgroup.IsFinitelyNormallyGenerated.map` carries finite normal generation
along.

## Scope

Nothing here is about lamps, wreath products or coronas.  What it does *not*
supply is the finite presentation of the free permutational precursor itself
(equation 54.17) -- that remains the outstanding piece for Lemma 54.3, and it
really is bookkeeping about one specific presentation.
-/

namespace GroupApproximation

namespace Tietze

universe u v

/-- **A finitely presented quotient of a finitely presented group has finitely
normally generated kernel.**  The converse of
`Group.IsFinitelyPresented.of_surjective`, and the form Lemma 54.3 consumes,
where neither the source nor the target is free.

Only the *generating* half of finite presentability of `E` is used: the relators
of `E` never appear. -/
theorem ker_isFinitelyNormallyGenerated {E : Type u} [Group E] {W : Type v}
    [Group W] [hE : Group.IsFinitelyPresented E] [Group.IsFinitelyPresented W]
    (f : E →* W) (hf : Function.Surjective f) :
    f.ker.IsFinitelyNormallyGenerated := by
  obtain ⟨n, φ, hφsurj, _⟩ := hE.out
  have hcomp : Function.Surjective (f.comp φ) := hf.comp hφsurj
  have hker : (f.comp φ).ker.IsFinitelyNormallyGenerated :=
    isFinitelyNormallyGenerated_ker (f.comp φ) hcomp
  have hmap : ((f.comp φ).ker).map φ = f.ker := by
    rw [← MonoidHom.comap_ker f φ]
    exact Subgroup.map_comap_eq_self_of_surjective hφsurj _
  rw [← hmap]
  exact hker.map hφsurj

end Tietze

end GroupApproximation
